  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14101062/assets/801bb98497bc275acda854447fffee30721bc54b2711ac632c2269f0c1eb64ed.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- There exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=-1$\n- $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As we can see from the diagram, there are no elements contained within $X$ that have an arrow to $-1\\in Y.$ Hence, $f(x)\\ne -1$ for all $x\\in X.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is false,  it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
